sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2024-memory-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "From examples such as frogs that seek out an odorant injected into the egg they developed from, Levin argues that engrams behave less like encoded memories and more like prompts, with much of the work (detecting novelty, remapping across scales, contextualizing and generalizing) done by the encoding and decoding process.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Engrams act more like prompts decoded by an intelligent receiver than like encodings";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Levin M, “Self-Improvising Memory: A Perspective on Memories as Agential, Dynamically Reinterpreting Cognitive Glue”, Entropy (Basel, Switzerland) (2024), doi:10.3390/e26060481; section “3. Remapping Memories: Beyond Storage and Simple Modification”. Scope: Memory transfer phenomena in animals (frog eggs, Aplysia brain-extract transfer)"
}
